It's highly possible that your headaches are related to posture or screen time, given your description of spending long hours at a desk. Poor posture can lead to muscle tension, which may contribute to headaches. Similarly, prolonged screen time can cause eye strain and fatigue, potentially exacerbating headache symptoms. Adjusting your workspace to promote better posture and taking regular breaks to stretch and move around could make a noticeable difference. 

Consider implementing ergonomic adjustments to your workspace, such as positioning your monitor directly in front of you, at a comfortable distance, and at a height that allows you to gaze slightly downward. Additionally, ensuring your chair supports your back and promotes good posture can help reduce muscle strain.

Taking breaks every hour to stand up, stretch, and move around can also help reduce the risk of developing headaches related to prolonged sitting. Furthermore, practicing good eye care habits, such as following the 20-20-20 rule (looking away from your screen every 20 minutes to focus on something 20 feet away for 20 seconds), can help mitigate eye strain.

Monitoring your headaches after implementing these changes will help determine if they have a significant impact on your symptoms. If your headaches persist or worsen, it would still be advisable to consult with a healthcare professional for a comprehensive evaluation.

